in store.go | FAIL `refusal site without an exercised negative path: store.go:172` |\n22|| Census fails closed, funnel bypass w/ sentinel | `refuse(ErrPredecessorLink,…)` → `fmt.Errorf(\"%w: …\", ErrPredecessorLink)` | FAIL `refusals raised outside the refuse funnel: chain.go:192` |\n23|| Census fails closed, funnel bypass w/o sentinel | `fmt.Errorf(\"invalid session record: …\")` | KILLED behaviorally by `TestAppendEventRefusesLaterPredecessorMismatch` |\n24|| Alias audit against **live production** | rewrote chain.go's import to `cjson` + direct calls | clean (correct: alias direct-call is allowed) |\n25|| Alias audit, live var binding | `verify := canonicaljson.VerifyObjectIdentity` in chain.go | FAIL `chain.go:73:26: constructor … referenced outside direct-call position` |\n26|| G-A substring plant | new pkg `internal/xsessrepo/` calling Verify | FAIL, names `internal/xsessrepo/plant.go:6` — segment match, not substring ✓ |\n27|| G-A stale allowlist | moved `internal/sessrepo/` out of the tree | FAIL `sessrepo attestation exception is stale: no allowlisted call site remains` ✓ |\n28|| G-A unparseable | planted a broken `.go` mentioning the identifier | FAIL `an unparseable derivation proves nothing` ✓ |\n29|| G-A dot-import bare call | `import . canonicaljson` + `VerifyObjectIdentity(raw)` | FAIL, named ✓ |\n30|| G-A ordering claim, decode sites | genuine §5.5 provider-identity record → `CreateSession` | refused `session record schema \"urn:ax:schema:provider-identity\", want \"urn:ax:schema:session-record\"` — the schema gate, named ✓ |\n31|| Sequence boundary (G-B) | `> want` → `> want+1` and `> want+2` | both KILLED by `TestAppendEventRefusesSequenceGap` ✓ (want-1 killed by the repeat test) |\n32|| G-E fuzz skip | enumerated targets: canonicaljson, scalar, secconftest, traceability | none touched by this CR; the disclosure is honest and correct ✓ |\n33|| Repo gates | `go build ./…`, `go vet ./…`, `GOOS=windows go vet ./…`, `GOOS=windows go build ./…`, `gofmt -l internal/`, `go test ./… -count=1` (24 ok), `go test -race` on sessrepo/provhost/invcore/canonicaljson, `tracecheck` (contracts=60 acceptance=98), `go test ./internal/cigate` | all exit 0 / clean ✓ |\n34|\n35|`cmd/` does not exist, so the outcome's `gofmt -l internal/` is the whole surface.\n36|\n37|## F1 (blocking, G-A) — the ordering claim the allowlist rests on is false, measured\n38|\n39|`identity.go` now states, and `doc.go` / `LOGBOOK.md` / the outcome repeat, that\n40|the sessrepo Verify call sites \"attest session-record and session-event bindings\n41|**after a schema gate that refuses any other schema first**, so no transport gate\n42|and **no persistence path recomputes a provider-identity digest today**.\"\n43|\n44|There are three production call sites, not two:\n45|\n46|```\n47|internal/sessrepo/chain.go:73   decodeSessionRecord   — schema gate above it ✓\n48|internal/sessrepo/chain.go:100  decodeSessionEvent    — schema gate above it ✓\n49|internal/sessrepo/chain.go:286  loadSessionLocked     — NO schema gate; bytes come off disk\n50|```\n51|\n52|Driven, not reasoned. I planted the verbatim §5.5 provider-identity record as an\n53|event blob named by a session's chain index and called the production entry\n54|`GetRecord`:\n55|\n56|```\n57|GetRecord(planted provider-identity blob)\n58|  -> session event chain is corrupt: re-verify chained event 0 for session …: <nil>\n59|```\n60|\n61|That `<nil>` is the finding: `canonicaljson.VerifyObjectIdentity` returned **no\n62|error** — it resolved the provider-identity contract, recomputed the\n63|provider-identity omit-self digest, and matched it. The refusal came afterwards,\n64|from `field != SelfEventID`. So a persistence path does recompute a\n65|provider-identity digest today, with no schema gate in front of it.\n66|\n67|The record is still refused, so this is not an admission hole. It is a false\n68|universal written into a production bound comment, the package doc's authority\n69|split, the repository logbook, and the CR's headline finding — and the brief is\n70|explicit that the allowlist's safety \"rests entirely on that ordering claim.\"\n71|Either gate the load-path bytes on schema before Verify, or rewrite the claim\n72|around what is true (two entry sites gated by schema; one load-path site that\n73|re-verifies stored bytes and refuses any non-`event_id` self field) — and pin the\n74|rewritten claim with a test, because today nothing does (see F2).\n75|\n76|Related, non-blocking: the refusal message at chain.go:288 formats `%v` on an\n77|`err` that is `nil` on two of its three clauses, so the diagnostic reads\n78|`re-verify chained event 0 …: <nil>` for both the wrong-self-field and the\n79|wrong-digest cases. Three distinct clauses share one message and one census row.\n80|\n81|## F2 (blocking, G-B + G-D) — \"12 applied, 12 killed, 0 survivors\" does not survive contact\n82|\n83|README ships that number as a repository claim. I ran 11 behavioral mutants in\n84|the same classes the battery claims (narrowing on gates). **Five survived the\n85|full committed package suite**, all of them on the load and recovery paths the\n86|battery never touches:\n87|\n88|| Mutant | Site | Narrowing | Result |\n89||---|---|---|---|\n90|| M1a | chain.go:286 | drop `field != SelfEventID` only | **SURVIVED** |\n91|| M1b | chain.go:286 | drop `digest.String() != indexed.EventID` only | **SURVIVED** |\n92|| M2 | chain.go `writeExclusive` | `O_EXCL` → `O_TRUNC` | **SURVIVED** |\n93|| M6 | sessrepo.go `resumeCreateLocked` | stored-vs-retried byte equality bypassed | **SURVIVED** |\n94|| M7 | chain.go:278 | drop `record.sessionID != sessionID` clause | **SURVIVED** |\n95|| M3 | `installEventBlob` disagreement | narrowed | KILLED (`…DisagreeingDigestPathBytes`) |\n96|| M4 / M4b | gap arm `> want+1` / `> want+2` | narrowed | KILLED |\n97|| M5 | `asciiFold` `Z`→`T` | narrowed | KILLED |\n98|| M8 | chain.go:278 drop `recordID != chain.RecordID` | narrowed | KILLED **by the census only** — `TestLoadRefusesDetachedChainIndex` still passes, satisfied by a downstream `checkAppend` arm, so the test does not witness the clause it names |\n99|\n100|Kill rate on my sample: 5 of 11. The battery's 12/12 is a true statement about\n101|twelve mutants that were aimed at the arms which already had tests.\n102|\n103|**M1b has a real failure scenario, and I built the witness.** Two events appended\n104|through `AppendEvent`, then the two blob *files* swapped so each digest path\n105|holds the other event's bytes. Both blobs individually verify and both carry\n106|`SelfEventID`, so the only clause that can refuse is the digest comparison.\n107|\n108|- unmutated production: `ListEvents` → `session event chain is corrupt` ✓ (the\n109|  clause works)\n110|- under M1b: `ListEvents(swapped blobs) -> <nil>` — **content-addressed blob\n111|  substitution admitted** — while `go test ./internal/sessrepo/` is **ok**.\n112|\n113|A ~40-line test driving that vector through `ListEvents` reddens M1a, M1b and M8\n114|at once.\n115|\n116|**M2 is the append-only obligation itself.** `doc.go` says \"files are\n117|content-addressed and installed no-replace, so the chain is append-only **by\n118|construction**\"; README says \"installed no-replace\". Flip `O_EXCL` to `O_TRUNC`\n119|and nothing in the repository notices. It is load-bearing: the `sessrepo` mutex\n120|is per-process, and the suite itself models a second process as a second `Open`\n121|over one root — two processes calling `CreateSession` with different records for\n122|one session ID both pass `os.Stat(directory)`, and `O_EXCL` is the only thing\n123|that stops the second from overwriting `record.json`. AC row 4 names\n124|`TestCreateSessionRefusesDuplicateAndRewrite` and `…RefusesSequenceRepeat`, but\n125|those are stopped by the `Stat` and the sequence arm, never by `O_EXCL`.\n126|\n127|This is the DoD bullet \"Every gate ships at least one NARROWING mutant\" failing\n128|for two gates (the load-path corruption gate and the no-replace install gate),\n129|and it is the DoD/G-B bullet \"no production path can rewrite or delete an\n130|existing entry, **proven by a negative test**\" resting on absence-of-a-method\n131|plus an unwitnessed `O_EXCL`.\n132|\n133|## F3 (blocking, G-B) — an interrupted create bricks the session ID and the whole listing\n134|\n135|`CreateSession` performs four durable steps (`MkdirAll` dir → `writeExclusive`\n136|record → `MkdirAll` events → `writeChain`). Both wired crash points sit outside\n137|that span: `BeforeWrite`/`PointPrepareEnter` fires before the first byte and\n138|`AfterCommit`/`PointCommitApply` after the last. `resumeCreateLocked` exists for\n139|exactly the in-between window but handles only one of its two sub-windows\n140|(record written, chain missing) and is driven by a hand-planted directory, never\n141|by a crash point.\n142|\n143|The other sub-window — crash after the directory exists, before `record.json` —\n144|is unrecoverable. Driven through production entries:\n145|\n146|```\n147|CreateSession(retry)  -> session already exists: session 0198f4c8-…-1234567890ab already exists\n148|GetRecord(same id)    -> session event chain is corrupt: read chain index …: no such file or directory\n149|ListSessions()        -> session event chain is corrupt: read chain index …: no such file or directory\n150|```\n151|\n152|`CreateSession` reports a session that was never created as existing, and there\n153|is no entry that can clear it. Worse, `ListSessions` fails closed on the first\n154|corrupt directory — deliberate and documented — so one interrupted create takes\n155|down the listing surface for **every** session in the repository.\n156|\n157|Both DoD crash bullets are satisfied in letter (a fault before the durable write\n158|and a fault after it are both driven). The gap is that a multi-write operation\n159|was wired with a two-point vocabulary that structurally cannot express its own\n160|recovery window, and the resume logic written for that window is half-covered.\n161|\n162|## F4 (non-blocking, G-A) — the allowlist admits any directory named `sessrepo`\n163|\n164|`sessrepoLeaf := sep + \"sessrepo\" + sep` matched with `strings.Contains` is a\n165|segment match — the substring plant is correctly refused (P1 above) — but it\n166|admits **any** path segment named `sessrepo`, not the owning directory. Planting\n167|`internal/provhost/sessrepo/plant.go` with a production Verify call:\n168|\n169|```\n170|attestation scan: 132 production files, 5 parsed, 4 session-leaf call sites, 0 elsewhere\n171|PASS\n172|```\n173|\n174|Four allowlisted sites where the real leaf has three. The comment says \"the one\n175|directory whose production `VerifyObjectIdentity` calls the rewritten bound\n176|admits\". Anchor it to the module-relative path (`internal/sessrepo/`) so the\n177|allowlist means what it says.\n178|\n179|## F5 (non-blocking, G-C) — reuse is right for the five named owners; the \"no new path policy\" claim is not\n180|\n181|Against the brief's G-C list, `sessrepo` is clean: it reuses `environ`\n182|(`DecodeStrictObject`, `CheckUUIDv7`, `CheckUint53Bounds`), `canonicaljson`\n183|(`VerifyObjectIdentity`), `scalar` (`ParseDigest`, `ParseUUIDv7`,\n184|`ParseUUIDv4`), `secconftest` points/outcomes/`Fault`, and `invcore` census\n185|machinery, with no new decoder, no new bound check, and no new AST walker.\n186|`secprim` is genuinely not needed: session IDs pass UUIDv7 grammar before any\n187|join, and `ListSessions` skips symlinked entries via `DirEntry.IsDir()`.\n188|\n189|But the outcome's \"No new decoder, bound check, **path policy**, AST walker, or\n190|journal: atomicity is temp-file + fsync + rename + dir-fsync\" is not accurate.\n191|`internal/localstore` already owns that policy — \"the AX owner-local path layout\n192|and immutable SHA-256 blob sink\" — and this CR re-implements it:\n193|\n194|| sessrepo | localstore |\n195||---|---|\n196|| `store.go syncDirectory` | `paths.go:535 syncDirectory` — same body |\n197|| `writeAtomic` temp+fsync+rename+dir-fsync | `object_store.go` staged write + `syncDirectory` |\n198|| `writeExclusive` `O_EXCL` install | `projection.go:339` `O_EXCL` + `syncDirectory` |\n199|| `installEventBlob` content-addressed idempotent install | `ObjectStore.PutBlob` |\n200|| `Open(dataRoot)/sessions` layout | `ResolvePaths` / `PathClass` |\n201|\n202|The two copies already disagree: sessrepo skips the directory fsync on\n203|`runtime.GOOS == \"windows\"`, localstore does not. `localstore` has no production\n204|consumer yet, so I am not asking you to rewire this leaf through it — but say so\n205|as a stated bound with the reason, instead of claiming no new path policy exists.\n206|\n207|## What I did not re-run\n208|\n209|Full fuzz smoke (`-fuzztime=100x`) — I judged the producer's skip rationale and\n210|agree with it (no fuzz target and no fuzzed package is touched), so I did not\n211|substitute a run for the judgement. Catalog freshness and the six `cigate`\n212|contract selections ran only as part of the unmasked `go test ./...`, not as\n213|separate named invocations.\n214|\n215|## Rework scope\n216|\n217|1. Make the F1 claim true or make it accurate, and pin whichever you choose.\n218|2. Drive the three unwitnessed clauses at chain.go:278/286 and the `O_EXCL`\n219|   no-replace property, then re-run the battery with the load and recovery paths\n220|   in the denominator. Republish the README battery numbers from that run.\n221|3. Decide what an interrupted create before the record write must do, and wire a\n222|   crash point that can reach the window.\n223|4. Anchor the allowlist to `internal/sessrepo/`.\n224|5. Correct the \"no new path policy\" claim, or reuse `localstore`.\n225|\n226|Items 1 and 2 are the ones that block acceptance. 3 is a product decision on\n227|recovery semantics — if the answer is \"an operator repairs it\", that is a stated\n228|bound, not a fix, but it has to be stated."}}}
